  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 239 AM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe focus of the long-term period will be Hurricane Isaias and its \npotential impacts on the forecast area. The latest forecast cone \nfrom NHC progs landfall somewhere between the Mississippi coast and \nwestern portion of the Florida Panhandle. This positions north and \ncentral Georgia on the eastern side of the center of Isaias, where \nstrong winds, flooding rain, and tornadoes will all be on the table.\n\nWind Threat: High pressure will remain in place on Friday, having \nbuilt in over the Appalachians on Thursday and been reinforced with \ncold air damming (the \"wedge\"). A tightening pressure gradient is \nexpected at the surface across Georgia as Isaias moves northward and \npushes up against the high pressure area. Due to this tightened \ngradient, the area of strong winds is forecast to be larger on the \neast side of Isaias. As a result, at least some period of sustained \nwinds of 15-25 mph and gusts of 25-35 mph can be expected across \nnorth and central Georgia. Additionally, a developing upper-level \nlow will be moving eastward across the Southeast Friday into \nSaturday. This will likely accelerate the extra-tropical transition \n(ET) of Isaias and may bring potential for an initial increase in \nwinds in the immediate hours after Isaias has begun its ET. There is \nalso the potential for the development of a \"sting jet\" -- an \nacceleration of descending air that brings extreme localized winds. \nSome form of wind-related Watch issuance is very likely in the next \n24 hours or so.\n\nFlooding Rain Threat: Given strong tropical moisture advection as \nIsaias moves inland, widespread rainfall is expected early Saturday \nmorning through early Sunday morning. The convective rainbands from \nIsaias will be where the most efficient rainfall will be focused. \nThat said, it is futile to try to pin down where exactly the \nheaviest rain will occur, so residents anywhere in Georgia should \nprepare for the possibility of localized flash flooding and/or river \nflooding, should they find themselves under persistent rainbands. \nOur current forecast has widespread 2 to 3 inches through the \nweekend, backed by most of the ensemble guidance. Would not be \nsurprised to see hi-res guidance painting bullseyes/swaths of \ngreater amounts over the coming days as they try to resolve the \nrainbands.\n\nTornado Threat: The conceptual model of tropical storms and \nhurricanes holds that the front right quadrant of these systems have \nthe greatest potential for tornadoes. Given that the center of \nIsaias will very likely track to the west of Georgia, tornadoes will \nbe a possibility across parts of the forecast area -- especially \nalong the wedge boundary and outside of the cooler, stable wedge \nairmass.",
